course: dictionary
lesson: 11003
title: Context clues: wasteland
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: You can often work out a new word from the words around it. These are its context, and they leave clues. «Wetlands are not wasteland: they soak up floods, clean water, and store huge amounts of carbon.» The word not is the first clue. Wetlands are useful, so wasteland must be the opposite: land that is not useful or good for anything.
explain: Look at the other words too. Soak up, clean and store all sound helpful. A wasteland is the opposite: empty, ruined or not used, with rubble, weeds and rubbish.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Try "Wetlands are not empty, ruined land". Does it still make sense? Yes, so you have found the right meaning.
explain: A wasteland can also be a place or time with nothing good or interesting in it, as in «a cultural wasteland». The clues tell you which meaning fits.
fact: Wasteland is a noun. You can have a wasteland, or several wastelands.
fact: A wasteland is land that is empty, ruined or not used, such as land with rubble, weeds and rubbish.
fact: In stories and films, a wasteland is a bleak, dangerous place, often after a war or a disaster.
fact: Empty patches of wasteland in cities can hide broken glass, so they are not safe places to play.
fact: Useful phrases: «a barren wasteland», «an urban wasteland», «an icy wasteland».
exercise: «Wetlands are not wasteland: they soak up floods, clean water, and store huge amounts of carbon.» What does wasteland mean here? | *Useless, empty land that is not good for anything | A wet place that soaks up floods | A place where clean water is made | A busy park full of trees
exercise: Nadia sees an empty patch of land where buildings were knocked down, full of broken glass and rubbish. What is it? | *A wasteland | A playground for all children | A new library | A sports field with fresh grass
exercise: Which phrase describes a bleak place covered in ice? | a green meadow | *an icy wasteland | a busy market | a sunny beach
test: «The old factory site was a wasteland of weeds and rubble.» What does wasteland mean? | A bright, busy shop | A lake full of fish | A new garden full of flowers | *Empty, ruined land with weeds and rubble
test: Why should children not play on a wasteland in a city? | It is always too hot to play there | *It can hide broken glass and other dangers | Wastelands are only for animals to live in | The grass is too soft to run on
exam: In the phrase «a cultural wasteland», what does wasteland mean? | A busy square full of shops | A rich and lively city full of art | *A place or time with nothing good or interesting in it | A quiet garden with ancient trees
